{% comment %}
  blocks/product-bundle-tabs.liquid — the category tab strip, and the panels
  behind it.

  ## Why this is a block and not part of the section

  It used to be markup welded into `sections/product-bundle.liquid`: a picker
  column on the left, a tray on the right, and one `content_for 'blocks'` that
  could only ever mean "the categories". A merchant who wanted the tray above
  the picker on desktop, or the strip under the heading, or a second column of
  anything at all, had no way to say so.

  As a block it is one thing a merchant places, inside a **Group** like any
  other block, and the section is what it should have been from the start: a
  `<product-bundle>` with `{% content_for 'blocks' %}` in it. The arrangement
  becomes the merchant's, which is what the rest of this theme already assumes.

  ## Its children draw their own pills, and this block draws none

  `block.blocks` does not exist in Liquid: a block cannot read its children's
  settings, so this one cannot draw its Categories' pills. That is a real
  constraint and it has not changed.

  What changed is the conclusion. This block used to render an empty
  `role="tablist"` for `assets/collection-tabs.js` to fill from markup each
  Category published beside its panel — and every version of that hand-off ran
  through a copy of the pill's markup. A copy that comes back short says
  nothing: the label survived every time, because the module could rebuild it
  from `data-label` alone, and the icon never appeared once.

  A block cannot draw its children's pills; it can draw its own. So each
  Category renders its complete pill where it stands, in Liquid, and there is
  no strip element here at all — `assets/base.css` lays the pills out as a row
  with `display: contents` on the Category root and `order` on its children.
  Which one is open is a radio group read by `:checked`. Nothing is assembled
  by script, so nothing can arrive empty.

  ## The strip's own design lives here, not on the section

  Every number and colour the pills are made of is a setting on this block:
  the four colours, the border, the corners, the height, the type, the space
  between the pills and the space between the strip and the products.

  They used to be `section.settings`, under a **Category tabs** heading, and a
  merchant styling the strip was in one panel while the thing they were styling
  was three blocks away. Worse, there was no way to have two strips look
  different — the settings belonged to the section, so every strip in it had
  the same design by construction.

  They resolve through `--bundle-tab-*` custom properties, and the cascade does
  the choosing between three levels without any rule knowing which won:

    the pill's own   `style="--bundle-tab-background: …"`, from the Category
    the strip's      the same property here, from this block
    the scheme's     the fallback written into each `var()` in `base.css`

  A colour the merchant has not set is never emitted, so an unset level simply
  is not there and the next one applies. That is why an unset colour is absent
  rather than `transparent`: a property that exists but is empty invalidates
  the whole declaration, and the pill would lose its background rather than
  inherit one.

  What stayed on the section is what is not the strip: how many product rows
  are on screen, the card's height, the tier ladder, the tray's wording.

  ## Plain and Segmented are one strip

  Segmented is the pill chooser drawn as a switch — a filled track with the
  options sharing its width. It is a modifier rather than a second component:
  what changes is a background, a radius and how the pills divide the space.

  There is no track element, because nothing can wrap the pills — they are
  drawn by four different blocks. So the track is drawn *by* the pills: they
  sit flush with the track colour behind each one and only the two on the ends
  are rounded, which is what a segmented control is. `assets/base.css` has it.

  There is no marker element sliding between the names either. The selected
  pill is filled by `:checked`, which is the browser's own state, so nothing
  can look selected while a different category is open.

  Segmented is horizontal by definition: a filled track running down the side
  of a page with the options stacked in it is a menu, not a switch. The
  orientation is forced here rather than hidden behind a `visible_if`, so a
  merchant who picks Segmented on a strip that was vertical gets the strip they
  chose instead of a broken one.
{% endcomment %}

{%- liquid
  assign s = block.settings

  assign tabs_id = 'bundle-tabs-' | append: block.id

  comment
    ---- the arrangement ----

    Segmented forces horizontal; see the note above. Everything else follows
    the merchant's Direction, and below 990px `base.css` turns a vertical strip
    back into a scrolling row — a phone spends one line on the categories
    rather than the whole screen.
  endcomment
  assign strip_style = s.strip_style | default: 'plain'
  assign orientation = s.direction | default: 'horizontal'
  if strip_style == 'segmented'
    assign orientation = 'horizontal'
  endif

  assign classes = 'product-bundle__tabs product-bundle__tabs--' | append: orientation
  if strip_style == 'segmented'
    assign classes = classes | append: ' product-bundle__tabs--segmented'
  endif

  comment
    Two properties, not one, and for the reason `snippets/group.liquid` sets
    out at length: `flex` only means anything to a flex *item*, and this block
    is one only when what it sits in lays its children out in a flex container
    — which a Group does and a plain container does not. Each option writes
    both, they agree, and neither context is a special case.
  endcomment
  if s.width == 'fit'
    assign width_flex = '0 0 auto'
    assign width_size = 'fit-content'
  else
    assign width_flex = '1 1 0%'
    assign width_size = '100%'
  endif

  comment
    `none` is a zero width rather than `border-style: none`, so the pills keep
    the same box either way and choosing None does not resize the strip.
  endcomment
  assign tab_border_style = s.tab_border_style | default: 'solid'
  assign tab_border_width = 0
  if tab_border_style != 'none'
    assign tab_border_width = s.tab_border_width | default: 1 | plus: 0
  endif

  assign strip_label = s.label
  if strip_label == blank
    assign strip_label = 'products.bundle.categories' | t
  endif

  comment
    ---- the line under the strip ----

    There has always been one, and it was never a decision. `[role='tablist']`
    in `assets/base.css` draws `border-block-end: 1px solid var(--color-border)`
    on every tablist in the theme — the underlined tab bar the cart drawer and
    the mega menu are built from — and this strip carries `role="tablist"`
    because it is one. So the line arrived by inheritance, hard against the
    bottom of the pills with nothing between them, and no setting in this panel
    could move it, recolour it or switch it off.

    It is now declared from these three properties, and the space above it is
    the fourth. `assets/base.css` defaults the width to 0, so the inherited line
    is gone until a merchant asks for one here.

    Only for a horizontal, plain strip. A vertical rail's pills run down the
    side, so a line under the last one is a rule across the middle of the
    section; a segmented strip already has a filled track drawn round it, and a
    line under that is a track with a tail.
  endcomment
  assign underline_applies = true
  if orientation != 'horizontal' or strip_style == 'segmented'
    assign underline_applies = false
  endif

  assign underline_style = s.strip_underline | default: 'none'
  unless underline_applies
    assign underline_style = 'none'
  endunless

  comment
    ---- the motion ----

    Two movements, and they are two settings because they are two things the
    customer sees: the pill filling as it is chosen, and the products arriving
    under it. `blocks/tabs.liquid` has had both since it shipped — a marker that
    slides between the names and `tab-panel-rise` on the panel that is no longer
    hidden — and this strip had neither. Pressing a category swapped one flat
    colour for another and replaced the whole list in the same frame, which
    reads as the page reloading rather than as a response to the tap.

    Neither can be the FAQ strip's implementation, and the reason is the one the
    note at the top of this file keeps running into: there is no strip element
    and there are no tab buttons this block drew. A marker is an element
    measured against the tab it sits behind, so it needs both — a box to be
    absolutely positioned inside, and a script that can read every tab's
    rectangle. Here the pills are drawn by four different blocks and laid out by
    `order`, so a marker would have to slide *behind* three opaque capsules on
    its way across, which looks like a bug rather than a transition.

    So the same two beats, drawn where they can be drawn:

      the pill    its selected fill is a layer inside the capsule that grows
                  into place, rather than a background colour that is simply
                  a different colour on the next frame. `assets/base.css` has
                  the layer; each pill animates its own, so nothing has to know
                  where its neighbours are.

      the panel   a keyframe on the panel CSS has just stopped hiding — which
                  is exactly `tabs__panel:not([hidden])`, one state along. Going
                  from `display: none` restarts a CSS animation, so the reveal
                  is the trigger and there is nothing to reset.

    The panel's movement is a custom property holding a keyframe *name* rather
    than a class per option, so `base.css` has one rule for every choice and
    the option list can grow here without a second edit there. `none` is a real
    `animation-name` value, so switching it off needs no special case either.
  endcomment
  assign tab_motion = s.tab_animation | default: 'fill'
  assign panel_motion = s.panel_animation | default: 'rise'

  assign classes = classes | append: ' product-bundle__tabs--tab-' | append: tab_motion

  assign panel_keyframes = 'none'
  unless panel_motion == 'none'
    assign panel_keyframes = 'bundle-panel-' | append: panel_motion
  endunless

  comment
    One slider for both, because they are one gesture: the pill fills and the
    products arrive, and a strip where those are set to different lengths reads
    as two things happening rather than one. The pill takes the shorter share of
    it in `assets/base.css` — a fill that is still spreading after the list has
    settled is the selection lagging behind its own result.

    Zero rather than the slider when the pill's movement is off, so it still has
    one `transition` declaration and that declaration simply takes no time. A
    property that is sometimes absent and sometimes present is two code paths
    for one control.
  endcomment
  assign motion_speed = s.motion_speed | default: 420 | plus: 0

  assign tab_speed_source = motion_speed
  if tab_motion == 'none'
    assign tab_speed_source = 0
  endif
-%}

{%- capture tabs_style -%}
  {%- render 'spacing', block: s, declarations: true -%}

  --bundle-tabs-gap: {{ s.gap | default: 24 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --bundle-tabs-rail-width: {{ s.rail_width | default: 260 | plus: 0 }}px;

  {%- comment -%}
    ---- the motion ----

    Three properties and no second element. `--bundle-panel-animation` carries
    an `animation-name`, so `assets/base.css` has one rule for every option and
    a new movement is a keyframe there plus an option here — there is no class
    to add in two files. `none` is a valid `animation-name`, so Off is not a
    special case anywhere.

    The pill takes 70% of the slider: the fill is the smaller of the two
    movements and finishing first is what makes it read as the cause of the
    list arriving rather than as a second event beside it.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  --bundle-motion-speed: {{ motion_speed }}ms;
  --bundle-tab-speed: calc({{ tab_speed_source }}ms * 0.7);
  --bundle-panel-animation: {{ panel_keyframes }};

  {%- comment -%}
    ---- the line under the strip, and the space above it ----

    Both only on the strip the line is drawn on. A vertical rail has no bottom
    edge to underline and a segmented strip already has a track round it — see
    the note at the top — so neither the rule nor the room below it is written
    there, and choosing Vertical does not leave a stray 16px under the rail.

    The two-value device switch the rest of the theme uses: the mobile number
    defaults to half the desktop one rather than to the desktop one, because a
    phone spends that space on a screen a third as tall.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if underline_applies -%}
    {%- liquid
      assign underline_gap = s.strip_underline_gap | default: 16 | plus: 0
      assign underline_gap_half = underline_gap | divided_by: 2
      assign underline_gap_mobile = s.strip_underline_gap_mobile | default: underline_gap_half | plus: 0
    -%}
    --bundle-strip-underline-gap: {{ underline_gap }}px;
    --bundle-strip-underline-gap-mobile: {{ underline_gap_mobile }}px;

    {%- if underline_style != 'none' -%}
      --bundle-strip-border-width: {{ s.strip_underline_width | default: 1 | plus: 0 }}px;
      --bundle-strip-border-style: {{ underline_style }};
      {%- if s.strip_underline_color != blank -%}
        --bundle-strip-border-color: {{ s.strip_underline_color }};
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    ---- the track ----

    Only written in Segmented. In Plain there is no track, and a colour or a
    radius emitted for one would be a number on something that is not drawn.

    **Track padding** is gone with the track element. There is no box around the
    pills to pad any more — there cannot be, because the pills are drawn by four
    different blocks and nothing can wrap them — so the segmented look is the
    pills themselves: flush against each other, the track colour behind each,
    and only the two on the ends rounded. The radius is those ends' radius,
    which is what the setting has always described. A padding slider with
    nothing to pad is a setting that lies, so it is not offered.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if strip_style == 'segmented' -%}
    --bundle-tabs-track-radius: {{ s.track_radius | default: 50 | plus: 0 }}px;
    {%- if s.track_background != blank -%}
      --bundle-tabs-track-background: {{ s.track_background }};
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    ---- the pill's measurements ----

    Every number the drawing pins, written here rather than taken from a token
    another panel is free to change. The type was `var(--font-size-sm)` once —
    Theme settings › Typography › Detail size — so a store that had set that to
    16 got a 16px tab, wider labels, and a strip that no longer matched the
    design it was built from.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  --bundle-tab-height: {{ s.tab_height | default: 37 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --bundle-tab-radius: {{ s.tab_radius | default: 50 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --bundle-tab-padding-inline: {{ s.tab_padding_inline | default: 16 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --bundle-tab-gap: {{ s.tab_gap | default: 10 | plus: 0 }}px;
  {%- comment -%}
    The space between the glyph and the name, inside one pill — not the space
    between two pills, which is `--bundle-tab-gap` above. `assets/base.css` has
    read this property since the pill was drawn and nothing ever wrote it, so
    every strip in every store has had the 0.6rem fallback and no way to change
    it. The drawing sets the pair 6 apart, which is what that fallback is, so
    the default here changes nothing and the slider now moves something.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  --bundle-tab-icon-gap: {{ s.tab_icon_gap | default: 6 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --bundle-tab-font-size: {{ s.tab_font_size | default: 14 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --bundle-tab-font-weight: {{ s.tab_font_weight | default: '500' }};

  --bundle-tab-border-width: {{ tab_border_width }}px;
  {%- if tab_border_width > 0 -%}
    --bundle-tab-border-style: {{ tab_border_style }};
    {%- if s.tab_border_color != blank -%}
      --bundle-tab-border: {{ s.tab_border_color }};
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    ---- the four colours ----

    Each is emitted only when the merchant has set it, so an untouched strip
    falls through to the colour scheme exactly as it did before these settings
    existed. See the note at the top for why absence and not `transparent`.

    Four fields because they are four decisions: what an unselected pill is
    made of, and what the selected one becomes. Saying "selected" with opacity
    alone reads on a tinted panel and nowhere else — over a photograph, or on a
    dark track, the chosen name and the three behind it are the same colour at
    two strengths, and which one is chosen stops being obvious at a glance.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if s.tab_background != blank -%}
    --bundle-tab-background: {{ s.tab_background }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.tab_color != blank -%}
    --bundle-tab-color: {{ s.tab_color }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.tab_active_background != blank -%}
    --bundle-tab-active-background: {{ s.tab_active_background }};
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if s.tab_active_color != blank -%}
    --bundle-tab-active-color: {{ s.tab_active_color }};
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- comment -%}
  ---- the animation ----

  One target, and it is the panels element, because the strip is no longer a
  second one — the pills are laid out inside the panels element rather than in
  a box of their own. Naming the pills as well would nest a motion target
  inside a motion target, and naming the panels individually would leave a
  reveal's opacity on a panel that CSS is about to show, which is the one way
  to make a category open to an empty rectangle.

  `block_level` so a preset that splits text into words or letters is mapped
  onto the whole-element preset that moves the same way: a pill is one shape
  and a pill whose label arrives letter by letter is a pill coming apart.
{%- endcomment -%}
<motion-effect
  class="product-bundle__tabs-motion"
  style="--bundle-tabs-flex: {{ width_flex }}; --bundle-tabs-width: {{ width_size }};"
  {% render 'motion-attrs',
     kind: 'text',
     block: s,
     block_level: true,
     target: '.product-bundle__panels'
  %}
  {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
>
  {%- comment -%}
    `data-block-spacing` is not decoration. `snippets/spacing.liquid` with
    `declarations: true` returns the custom properties and nothing else — no
    `style=`, no hook — because the declarations have to go into this element's
    one `style` attribute alongside everything else here. `assets/base.css`
    applies block padding through `[data-block-spacing]`, so without the
    attribute the four properties land on an element no rule selects and every
    padding slider in the panel moves a value nothing reads.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <collection-tabs
    class="{{ classes }}"
    id="{{ tabs_id }}"
    data-block-spacing
    style="{{ tabs_style | strip_newlines | strip }}"
  >
    {%- comment -%}
      ---- there is no strip element any more ----

      There used to be an empty `role="tablist"` here for
      `assets/collection-tabs.js` to fill. Every Category published its pill as
      inert markup beside its panel and the module copied it in, because
      `block.blocks` does not exist in Liquid: this block cannot read its
      children's settings and so cannot draw their buttons.

      That constraint is real and it is not going away. What was wrong was the
      conclusion drawn from it — that the pills must therefore be *assembled* by
      script. Every route the module had into this element ran through a copy of
      the pill's markup, and a copy that comes back short says nothing: the
      label survived every time, because the module could rebuild that from
      `data-label` on its own, and the icon never did. One bug, three
      implementations, and the only way to stop writing it was to stop copying.

      A block cannot draw its children's pills. It can draw its own — so it
      does, in `blocks/_product-bundle-category.liquid`, where the icon is a
      `snippets/icon.liquid` SVG in the HTML the server sends, with nothing
      between rendering it and seeing it.

      The strip is then a row in a layout rather than an element:
      `.product-bundle__cat` is `display: contents`, so each Category's pill and
      panel are laid out by `.product-bundle__panels` below, and `order` puts
      every pill on the first line and every panel under them. The block comment
      in `assets/base.css` has the whole arrangement.

      Which category is open is a radio group read by `:checked` — no roving
      tabindex to maintain, no panel hidden by script, and the arrow keys are
      the browser's own. `collection-tabs.js` builds nothing now: it re-measures
      a carousel when its panel is revealed and follows the Theme Editor's block
      selection. The strip is complete and correct with the module absent.

      The strip's name moves onto the panels element, because that is what now
      contains the radios. `role="group"` rather than `radiogroup`: the radios
      are not this element's only children — the panels are in here too — and a
      `radiogroup` promises a container of nothing but options.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div
      class="product-bundle__panels"
      data-ref="panels"
      role="group"
      aria-label="{{ strip_label | escape }}"
    >
      {% content_for 'blocks' %}
    </div>
  </collection-tabs>
</motion-effect>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "t:blocks.product_bundle_tabs.name",
  "tag": null,
  "blocks": [
    { "type": "_product-bundle-category" }
  ],
  "settings": [
    { "type": "paragraph", "content": "t:blocks.product_bundle_tabs.paragraph" },

    { "type": "header", "content": "t:blocks.group.headers.layout" },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "strip_style",
      "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.strip_style.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.product_bundle_tabs.settings.strip_style.info",
      "options": [
        { "value": "plain", "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.strip_style.options__1.label" },
        { "value": "segmented", "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.strip_style.options__2.label" }
      ],
      "default": "plain"
    },
    {
      "type": "color",
      "id": "track_background",
      "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.track_background.label",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.strip_style == 'segmented' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "track_radius",
      "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.track_radius.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.product_bundle_tabs.settings.track_radius.info",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 50,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 50,
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.strip_style == 'segmented' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "color",
      "id": "tab_background",
      "label": "t:sections.product_bundle.settings.tab_background.label"
    },
    {
      "type": "color",
      "id": "tab_color",
      "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.tab_color.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.tab_color.info"
    },
    {
      "type": "color",
      "id": "tab_active_background",
      "label": "t:sections.product_bundle.settings.tab_active_background.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.product_bundle_tabs.settings.tab_active_background.info"
    },
    {
      "type": "color",
      "id": "tab_active_color",
      "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.tab_active_color.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.product_bundle_tabs.settings.tab_active_color.info"
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "direction",
      "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.direction.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.direction.info",
      "options": [
        { "value": "horizontal", "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.direction.options__2.label" },
        { "value": "vertical", "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.direction.options__1.label" }
      ],
      "default": "horizontal",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.strip_style != 'segmented' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "rail_width",
      "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.rail_width.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.rail_width.info",
      "min": 140,
      "max": 400,
      "step": 10,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 260,
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.direction == 'vertical' and block.settings.strip_style != 'segmented' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "gap",
      "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.gap.label",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 80,
      "step": 2,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 24
    },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "width",
      "label": "t:blocks.group.settings.width.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.group.settings.width.info",
      "options": [
        { "value": "fill", "label": "t:blocks.group.settings.width.options__2.label" },
        { "value": "fit", "label": "t:blocks.group.settings.width.options__1.label" }
      ],
      "default": "fill"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "label",
      "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.label.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.label.info"
    },

    { "type": "header", "content": "t:shared.appearance.header" },
    {
      "type": "select",
      "id": "tab_border_style",
      "label": "t:blocks.tabs.settings.tab_border_style.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.product_bundle_tabs.settings.tab_border_style.info",
      "options": [
        { "value": "none", "label": "t:shared.options.none" },
        { "value": "solid", "label": "t:shared.options.solid" },
        { "value": "dashed", "label": "t:shared.border.style.options__2.label" }
      ],
      "default": "solid"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "tab_border_width",
      "label": "t:shared.border.thickness.label",
      "min": 1,
      "max": 6,
      "step": 1,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 1,
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.tab_border_style != 'none' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "color",
      "id": "tab_border_color",
      "label": "t:shared.border.color.label",
      "visible_if": "{{ block.settings.tab_border_style != 'none' }}"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "tab_radius",
      "label": "t:blocks.product_bundle_tabs.settings.tab_radius.label",
      "info": "t:blocks.product_bundle_tabs.settings.tab_radius.info",
      "min": 0,
      "max": 50,
      "step": 1,
      "unit": "px",
      "default": 50
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
